t {:answer/id #uuid "217244d5-85da-5c1f-9377-eeaa16debb00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300124} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8515511-0d07-53a2-bb49-746ea67dfa91" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301768}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a473799-9dcd-5e6d-9f92-4a1b0e38bad7" :question/math-academy-id "q-326304" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=4t-{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{2}+3,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}$,\\,y$\n{{field-3}}{{field-4}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4t-{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-({t}^{2}-4t) \\\\  & =-({t}^{2}-4t+4)+4 \\\\  & =-(t-2)^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-(t-2)^{2}+4\\le 4,$ we conclude that the range of $x$ is\n\n$$\nx\\le 4.\n$$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that ${t}^{2}+3\\ge 3.$ Consequently, the range of $y$ is\n\n$$\ny\\ge 3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "183eace4-e338-5bc7-9a8b-c34f12c92bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "183eace4-e338-5bc7-9a8b-c34f12c92bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300063} :answer-field/id #uuid "1468603f-a543-5515-a97a-0150fa375a7b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301293} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da881d5a-28d4-5e93-a51d-56a0fa28f022" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301045}] :answer-field/correct {