 -1 & \\le x\\le \\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-1\\le x\\le \\frac{5}{3}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & \\le \\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{7}{3} & \\le \\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{7}{3}\\le \\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{29}{6} & \\le y\\le \\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{29}{6}\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{6}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05970e26-5821-56d5-9192-cd54ac992296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\le x\\le 5,\\,-9\\le y\\le -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336077} {:answer/id #uuid "66a3960b-62dc-53fd-84aa-3678e0fb86aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le 13,\\,3\\le y\\le 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336947} {:answer/id #uuid "712b9c6e-788b-5ba0-8229-df8fca209a1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\le x\\le 6,\\,-13\\le y\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337040} {:answer/id #uuid "8f5bbdd7-51a4-577f-a287-db618f622b13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 5,\\,-7\\le y\\le 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337327} {:answer/id #uuid "fa563dbb-82b3-57bc-8de5-0041f39c1575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\le x\\le 0,\\,1\\le y\\le 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "712b9c6e-788b-5ba0-8229-df8fca209a1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\le x\\le 6,\\,-13\\le y\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337040} :answer-field/id #uuid "f957fa19-2286-54e9-bed6-f7be3613a470" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338716}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1fb3f2b5-56e5-553e-8562-5d6280836898" :question/math-academy-id "q-70523" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,\\,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -7,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -1+5 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5\\le 1+5 \\\\ 4 & \\le x\\le 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $4\\le x\\le 6.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -6 & \\le 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 6 \\\\ -6-7 & \\le 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -7\\le 6-7 \\\\ -13 & \\le y\\le -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-13\\le y\\le -1.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $4\\le x\\le 6$ and $-13\\le y\\le -1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342038 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f6b6688-7ebd-5b25-83b7-5c07ad7c688a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\le x\\le 5,\\,1\\le y\\le 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339270} {:answer/id #uuid "25d8d7af-c8e4-5ebb-980e-a55d3a4cd6d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 3,\\,-5\\le y\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339454} {:answer/id #uuid "3320f739-78f0-51e9-9081-4e93b3163fc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\le x\\le 4,\\,-1\\le y\\le 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339582} {:answer/id #uuid "94fae60d-72de-52be-8e5a-de4200f76d69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\le x\\le 6,\\,-5\\le y\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340469} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd74067-cc9b-5848-b108-229097ff8d94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,-5\\le y\\le 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25d8d7af-c8e4-5ebb-980e-a55d3a4cd6d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 3,\\,-5\\le y\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339454} :answer-field/id #uuid "b483317f-a4a2-5639-8bcf-ae196dafce5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341697}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f2f3822c-027f-5b78-99af-6944b4b3bf81" :question/math-academy-id "q-73626" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ 2 & \\text{ }\\ge -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -2 \\\\ -2 & \\le -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ 1-2 & \\le 1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1+2 \\\\ -1 & \\le x\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-1\\le x\\le 3.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -2 & \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ -2-3 & \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3\\le 2-3 \\\\ -5 & \\le y\\le -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-5\\le y\\le -1.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $-1\\le x\\le 3$ and $-5\\le y\\le -1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342042 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fcf5022-4812-5cf2-a745-37302fcd5dc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le -1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339275} {:answer/id #uuid "4915a118-a0a0-5db9-914a-1721d3b42565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\le x\\le 1,\\,-2\\le y\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339784} {:answer/id #uuid "b457cadc-b9f6-5c2c-9f2c-42f3a633bbd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340764} {:answer/id #uuid "d4206d0e-3e82-51c1-8e8c-9cad7033e6f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\le x\\le 3,\\,-1\\le y\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341005} {:answer/id #uuid "e66ef8b5-6026-532a-a34b-a4ed72b384c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b457cadc-b9f6-5c2c-9f2c-42f3a633bbd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340764} :answer-field/id #uuid "c59dee8b-2e72-5994-98ef-02b4aa2af213" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341726}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbbe4cfc-6544-53d2-868c-0f7ad468266a" :question/math-academy-id "q-73632" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{3}{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -2 & \\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ -2+1 & \\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1\\le 2+1 \\\\ -1 & \\le x\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-1\\le x\\le 3.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\le \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2} & \\le \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{3}{2}\\le \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & \\le y\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $1\\le y\\le 2.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $-1\\le x\\le 3$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400121 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "befcfe4a-abd3-5984-a1a3-1a1bf2b0cd72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le 0,\\,-3\\le y\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400123} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee13255-e4e9-52e6-b06e-d0aeddb3bb38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400124} {:answer/id #uuid "c3236814-4c23-5fc9-a82d-b9432370c830" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-3\\le y\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400125} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd316cc-7219-5b56-b38c-fa0c32ea3d94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 2,\\,-2\\le y\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400126} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb2b3dc-0e10-5966-a703-a0088b75ec08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fb2b3dc-0e10-5966-a703-a0088b75ec08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400127} :answer-field/id #uuid "806b0a66-23a8-530b-9fd2-35a679c8dd3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400122}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42535d46-cb00-5afc-82ab-e62c0ea06521" :question/math-academy-id "q-123773" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\ge -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -1 \\\\ -1 & \\le -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ 1-1 & \\le 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1+1 \\\\ 0 & \\le x\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $0\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -1+2 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\le 1+2 \\\\ 1 & \\le y\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $1\\le y\\le 3.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le 3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400128 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64998bfc-bfc7-5d3e-8ee6-300d3a38797d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,-4\\le y\\le -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400130} {:answer/id #uuid "42492b12-0052-541d-8cee-a36023e92984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,-3\\le y\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400131} {:answer/id #uuid "2c4ef62b-01d3-5f37-819a-bf2a72b19db1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,-3\\le y\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400132} {:answer/id #uuid "79eb9da7-f01c-5dfb-a236-47a585231977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,2\\le y\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400133} {:answer/id #uuid "617de8a7-03f5-5305-95c8-3f2f2a0cf8cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\le x\\le 3,\\,2\\le y\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79eb9da7-f01c-5dfb-a236-47a585231977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,2\\le y\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400133} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef6815f3-6a4d-5946-a00b-a698526a94b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400129}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df6cb91b-a536-5526-9dd6-80b470cc8a61" :question/math-academy-id "q-123775" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\le \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -1+3 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\le 1+3 \\\\ 2 & \\le y\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $2\\le y\\le 4.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}$ and $2\\le y\\le 4.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation Defined in Terms of Trigonometric Functions"}]}
